.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt SIGNALFD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm signalfd ,
.Nm signalfd4
.Nd signals read through a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/signalfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn signalfd "int fd" "const sigset_t *mask" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn signalfd ,
whose C library function calls
.Fn signalfd4 ,
number 327 on i386 and 289 on x86-64, or with no flags
.Fn signalfd ,
number 321 on i386 and 282 on x86-64, makes a descriptor that reads the signals of
.Fa mask ,
or with
.Fa fd
a signalfd already open, gives it
.Fa mask
in place of its own.
The mask is eight bytes;
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGSTOP
are left out of it, as Linux leaves them.
The flags are
.Dv SFD_NONBLOCK
and
.Dv SFD_CLOEXEC .
The signals are blocked by the caller, so that they wait for the read.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
takes the pending signals of the mask, the thread's own first and then
the process's, as
.Xr sigtimedwait 2
takes them, as many as fit, each a 128-byte
.Vt struct signalfd_siginfo :
the number,
.Va ssi_errno ,
the code, and the fields of the layout Linux's
.Fn siginfo_layout
gives the signal and code, as Linux's
.Fn signalfd_copyinfo
fills them: a sender's pid and uid in
.Va ssi_pid
and
.Va ssi_uid ,
with a queued signal's value in
.Va ssi_ptr
and its low word in
.Va ssi_int ;
a child's pid, uid, status and times in
.Va ssi_pid ,
.Va ssi_uid ,
.Va ssi_status ,
.Va ssi_utime
and
.Va ssi_stime ;
a timer's id, overrun and value in
.Va ssi_tid ,
.Va ssi_overrun ,
.Va ssi_ptr
and
.Va ssi_int ;
a band and a descriptor in
.Va ssi_band
and
.Va ssi_fd ;
a fault's address in
.Va ssi_addr ,
with an MCE's address bits in
.Va ssi_addr_lsb ;
or a seccomp
.Dv SIGSYS Ns 's
call address, number and architecture in
.Va ssi_call_addr ,
.Va ssi_syscall
and
.Va ssi_arch .
With none pending it waits for one, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
when non-blocking.
The wait leaves the caller's signal mask as it is: a signal of the mask
that another thread does not block goes to that thread, or ends the
process at its default action, and the read sees only a signal that no
thread takes, as on Linux; a program blocks the mask's signals in every
thread.
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report the descriptor readable while a signal of its mask is pending for
the caller.
Every signal sent to the process or to one of its threads wakes each
thread that reads or polls a signalfd, whichever thread the signal goes
to.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:[signalfd] .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The descriptor, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is neither -1 nor an open descriptor.
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa mask
or a read's buffer is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa fd
is not a signalfd, the mask's size is not eight, a flag is unknown, or a
read's buffer is shorter than a record.
.It Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the descriptor.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr sigtimedwait 2 ,
.Xr timerfd 2
